The 6 Subquery Patterns You Should Remember

These 15 questions basically teach you these patterns:

1. Single-value subquery
WHERE price > (
    SELECT AVG(price)
    FROM sales_orders
);

Used when the subquery returns one value.

2. IN
WHERE customer_name IN (
    SELECT customer_name
    FROM sales_orders
    GROUP BY customer_name
);

Used when the subquery can return multiple values.

3. ALL
WHERE price > ALL (
    SELECT price
    FROM sales_orders
    WHERE category = 'Furniture'
);

Means greater than every value.

4. ANY
WHERE price > ANY (
    SELECT price
    FROM sales_orders
    WHERE category = 'Furniture'
);

Means greater than at least one value.

5. Correlated subquery
SELECT *
FROM sales_orders s
WHERE price = (
    SELECT MAX(price)
    FROM sales_orders
    WHERE category = s.category
);

The inner query depends on the current row of the outer query.

6. DISTINCT
SELECT DISTINCT customer_name

